= Abstract <chap:abstract>

High-quality Digital Terrain Models (DTMs) are increasingly available across Europe through regional, and local mapping and research programmes. However, these datasets are created by many different organisations, resulting in differences in Coordinate Reference Systems (CRS), resolution, Vertical CRS (VCRS), data processing, and formats. These differences make it challenging to create one unified seamless raster across large cross-border regions.

This project aims to answer how a harmonized cross-border Digital Elevation Model (DEM) of the Rhine catchment area can be created in an automated manner. To achieve this, a pipeline has been developed which automatically handles data harmonisation, processing, and visualisation. The pipeline acquires, reprojects, vertically transforms, resamples, and aggregates DTM datasets into a common representation. Additional processing steps include interpolation of missing data, the generation of a rivermask, and semi-automated border analysis between neighboring datasets.

The resulting DTM covers six countries, and multiple German federal states within the Rhine catchment area. To evaluate the consistency of the harmonised dataset, neighboring DTMs are compared using elevation difference rasters, statistical analysis, coregistration, and a set of diagnostic plots and DTMs. The analysis identified several recurring categories of disagreement artifacts, including interpolation differences, ground filtering approaches, temporal differences, water modelling, and residual artifacts introduced during LiDAR data aquisition and processing. Most disagreements are the result of the input datasets, and not due to the harmonisation pipeline itself. Most disagreements can be resolved by reprocessing border point clouds using consistent temporal data selection and processing approaches.

The project demonstrates that harmonized cross-border DTM data can be generated automatically from heterogeneous elevation data, while retaining the origional data without applying methods such as cross-border smoothing, which is applied in existing approaches and products. Furthermore, the results highlight the challenges that remain for harmonising the cross-border DEMs, and provide recommendation for improving the interoperability of data within the Rhine catchment area.